<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and restrict a finite inverse-power potential with
arbitrary real coefficients to a line. Combine terms having the same
projected centre and squared height <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, delete classes whose
coefficient sum is zero, and let <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of remaining classes.
We prove the following dichotomy. If <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, exact cancellation occurs if
and only if every class sum vanishes, and every ordinary point of the
original domain is critical. If <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at most <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math>
critical points: when all effective heights are positive the zeros are
counted with analytic multiplicity, while in the presence of effective
sources on the line the assertion is one global distinct-point bound.
This proves the signed line conjecture of
Gabrielov–Novikov–Shapiro, valid throughout their range and in fact for
every <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The structural input is a projective paired Haar
theorem: for finite <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the full <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space
<math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum L_{j}/Q_{j}^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>β</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum L_{j}/Q_{j}^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, with pairwise nonproportional positive-definite
binary quadratics and arbitrary real linear numerators, has at most
<math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math> projective zeros counted with multiplicity. For positive charges,
the substitution <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> proves Conjecture 3 of
Edelsbrunner–Fillmore–Oliveira throughout its stated range <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and extends the same conclusion to every <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, an
explicit positive <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-charge configuration attains <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> simple critical
points.</p>
  
</div>
