<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On a prescribed line, a signed inverse-power
restriction has the form</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\eta_{i}\bigl((t-a_{i})^{2}+b_{i}^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\eta_{i}\bigl((t-a_{i})^{2}+b_{i}^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A point <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is ordinary if <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The question is to bound the zeros of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the ordinary domain.
This is a tangential critical-point problem, not the unrestricted
electrostatic equilibrium problem: a critical point of a line restriction
need not be a critical point of the ambient potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first issue is algebraic rather than analytic.
Terms arising from distinct charge locations can induce the same restricted kernel.
Such terms must be combined, and a
class whose coefficient sum vanishes must be removed. We make this
reduction canonical below and distinguish the original domain from the possibly
larger reduced domain obtained by filling canceled punctures.
The resulting number <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of nonzero effective kernels, rather than the
number <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> of terms in the original representation, is the natural parameter in the theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Gabrielov, Novikov, and Shapiro formulated their line-restriction conjecture
for arbitrary real coefficients and <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; it is
Conjecture 1.9 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Its literal wording permits
restrictions that vanish identically by exact cancellation, so any finite bound
must treat that case separately from the <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> case.
Edelsbrunner, Fillmore, and Oliveira (EFO) later stated the
positive-charge specialization for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> as their Conjecture 3
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> (where they attribute this conjecture to Gabrielov, Novikov, and Shapiro).
Their coefficients are positive,
and their exponent is related to ours by <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>; the EFO conjecture
is therefore a positive specialization, not an equivalent signed
formulation. We prove the corrected signed statement for every
<math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the positive statement for every <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The structural theorem is a projective Haar bound for a paired family.
A line derivative determines one linear numerator over each quadratic denominator; it belongs to
the larger paired space obtained by allowing an arbitrary real linear numerator over each denominator.
We prove that the full <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space has
the <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math> zero bound on <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with multiplicity. This is a global
Haar/extended-Chebyshev assertion for the full space;
it does not assert that every ordering of the generators
forms an extended complete Chebyshev system (ECT-system).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our terminology follows the classical theory of Tchebycheff and Haar spaces
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Several adjacent results do not directly imply
the paired theorem. Strict positive definiteness of inverse-multiquadric
interpolation matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> is weaker than total positivity
and does not control zeros at arbitrary test nodes; this distinction is
made explicit in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, with classical
total-positivity background in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Prescribed-pole
Cauchy–Vandermonde ECT systems cover the rational endpoint <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but not the
paired family for <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> needed here <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>; known total-positivity
results for a Cauchy kernel concern a different one-parameter kernel
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Positive mixtures of two Cauchy densities form a
previously classified special case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. To our knowledge, the
global projective Haar bound for the full variable-quadratic paired space has
not appeared before. The proof is a new application of convex-curve methods
of M. Z. Shapiro and Anisov after a family-specific Wronskian and
rational-normal endpoint analysis. For comparison, the compatible fixed-Spin
convention recorded by Saldanha–B. Shapiro and Alves is also documented;
this is not a claim of a new general theory of Chebyshev systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof has two structural steps. Gegenbauer generating functions first
identify the paired Wronskian with a gradient-evaluation determinant. A
vanishing determinant would produce too many critical points of a
generalized-axisymmetric harmonic polynomial; a planar index theorem rules
this out. An exponent homotopy then connects the locally convex paired
curve to the rational normal curve.
Anisov’s closed-projective
loss-of-convexity theorem supplies the global step;
the fixed-Spin
component formulation recorded by Saldanha–B. Shapiro and Alves gives a
compatible but non-load-bearing interval description
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Smooth signed
restrictions follow directly. Effective sources on the line are treated by
one globally coordinated two-sided smoothing, not by adding componentwise
estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In independent work submitted shortly after the first version of the present paper,
Oliveira <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> proves the same <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math> bound for distinct critical points of signed line restrictions for every <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both approaches use paired families and planar index arguments, but Oliveira uses duality with an auxiliary potential, whereas we use Gegenbauer jets and projective convexity.
Our smoothing argument also yields the refined singular derivative bound <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-r-1</annotation></semantics></math> in Remark <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Remark 6.4 (Refined singular bound). ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, where <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of effective poles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Reduction and main results</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the data in
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The original regular domain is</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{a_{i}:b_{i}=0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{a_{i}:b_{i}=0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i\sim j\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})=(a_{j},b_{j}^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\sim j\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})=(a_{j},b_{j}^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Because the heights <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen nonnegative, this is equivalent to equality
of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},b_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},b_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is the invariant visible in the
restricted kernel.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℭ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of equivalence classes.
For each <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathfrak{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℭ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathfrak{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{C},b_{C}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{C},b_{C}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the common value of <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},b_{i}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, and define</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\eta}_{C}=\sum_{i\in C}\eta_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\eta}_{C}=\sum_{i\in C}\eta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{C}_{*}=\{C\in\mathfrak{C}:\widehat{\eta}_{C}\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℭ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℭ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{C}_{*}=\{C\in\mathfrak{C}:\widehat{\eta}_{C}\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and put
<math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|\mathfrak{C}_{*}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ℭ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|\mathfrak{C}_{*}|</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, reindex the active classes by
<math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote their nonzero coefficients and distinct
parameters by <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{j},b_{j}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{j},b_{j}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following sum
is understood to be empty. Define the reduced extension</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}(t)=\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_{j}\bigl((t-a_{j})^{2}+b_{j}^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}(t)=\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_{j}\bigl((t-a_{j})^{2}+b_{j}^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">on</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\rm red}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{a_{j}:b_{j}=0,\ 1\leq j\leq m\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{a_{j}:b_{j}=0,\ 1\leq j\leq m\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">Then</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\rm orig}\subseteq D_{\rm red},\qquad F_{\alpha}=F_{\rm red}\quad\hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>orig</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>red</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>red</mi></msub><mtext>on </mtext><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>orig</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}\subseteq D_{\rm red},\qquad F_{\alpha}=F_{\rm red}\quad\hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Thus reduction fills only punctures produced by classes that cancel
exactly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the preceding notation,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\widehat{\eta}_{C}=0\ \hbox{for every }C\in\mathfrak{C}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad F_{\alpha}\equiv 0\ \hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for every </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℭ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">on </mtext><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>orig</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\widehat{\eta}_{C}=0\ \hbox{for every }C\in\mathfrak{C}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad F_{\alpha}\equiv 0\ \hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, exactly one of the following alternatives holds.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the zero function, and every point
of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is critical.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and every effective height is positive, then
</span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}=\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}=\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{t\in\mathbb{R}:\,F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)=0}\operatorname{ord}_{t}(F_{\rm red}^{\prime})\leq 2m-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t\in\mathbb{R}:\,F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)=0}\operatorname{ord}_{t}(F_{\rm red}^{\prime})\leq 2m-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and at least one effective height is zero, then the
single global distinct-point estimate</span></p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{I\in\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})}\#\{t\in I:F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)=0\}\leq 2m-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{I\in\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})}\#\{t\in I:F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)=0\}\leq 2m-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">holds. Here </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes the set of connected components of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
No multiplicity assertion is made in this singular case.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the two nonzero alternatives, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence</span></p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1\leq 2\ell-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1\leq 2\ell-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The critical points on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math> are a subset of those counted on
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the applicable bound also holds on the original
domain.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scope of the dichotomy)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">No sign, total-charge, moment, genericity, or off-line assumption occurs in
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. It includes mixed signs, zero total charge,
vanishing lower moments, degenerate smooth critical points, sources on the
restricting line, and every finite <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
An effective pole–that is, a point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in the reduced data–lies outside both <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> and is never counted as critical.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A real binary quadratic form <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive definite</em> when
<math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(u,v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(u,v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\neq(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\neq(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. For pairwise
nonproportional such forms <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, put</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\beta}(Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n})=\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{L_{i}(u,v)}{Q_{i}(u,v)^{\beta}}:L_{i}\in\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\beta}(Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n})=\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{L_{i}(u,v)}{Q_{i}(u,v)^{\beta}}:L_{i}\in\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the two-dimensional space of homogeneous real linear forms <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Au+Bv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Au+Bv</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the unit circle, these expressions are smooth and anti-periodic, and
therefore define sections of the tautological real line bundle on
<math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their zero sets and local orders descend to <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; a zero
at infinity is measured in the complementary affine chart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Paired Haar theorem)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> be finite, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be pairwise
nonproportional positive-definite real binary quadratic forms. Then
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\beta}(Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\beta}(Q_{1},\ldots,Q_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>, and every nonzero
member has at most <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> zeros on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, counted with multiplicity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This is a global projective Haar/extended-Chebyshev theorem for the full
paired space. Pairwise nonproportionality is the only distinctness
hypothesis, arbitrary real linear numerators are allowed, and no ordering of
the quadratics is used. Rank-one quadratics are excluded and will be
handled separately in the singular part of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Paired Haar theorem). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> in
<a href="#S3" title="3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S4" title="4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5" title="5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>; the signed theorem is
then proved in <a href="#S6" title="6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Its physical consequences and
examples appear in <a href="#S7" title="7. Point-charge consequences ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S8" title="8. Examples and positive optimality ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an arbitrary point of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, choose a local affine coordinate
<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> that vanishes there, and use <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,s</annotation></semantics></math> as a numerator basis. Dividing
each quadratic by its positive value at <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(s)=1+2X_{i}s+Y_{i}s^{2},\qquad Y_{i}&gt;X_{i}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(s)=1+2X_{i}s+Y_{i}s^{2},\qquad Y_{i}&gt;X_{i}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The normalized pairs <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{i},Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{i},Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct: equality of two normalized
quadratics would make the original forms proportional. The local paired
family is</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(s)^{-\beta},\qquad sq_{i}(s)^{-\beta},\qquad i=1,\ldots,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(s)^{-\beta},\qquad sq_{i}(s)^{-\beta},\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Put <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\beta-1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\beta-1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The Gegenbauer generating function is</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-2zt+t^{2})^{-\lambda}=\sum_{r\geq 0}C_{r}^{\lambda}(z)t^{r};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-2zt+t^{2})^{-\lambda}=\sum_{r\geq 0}C_{r}^{\lambda}(z)t^{r};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">see DLMF 18.12.4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Sec. 4.7]</cite>.
Here <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{r}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{r}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Gegenbauer polynomial of degree <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, in the normalization determined by this generating function.
With <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=X/\sqrt{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>Y</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=X/\sqrt{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=-\sqrt{Y}\,s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>Y</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=-\sqrt{Y}\,s</annotation></semantics></math>, it gives</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+2Xs+Ys^{2})^{-\lambda}=\sum_{r\geq 0}(-1)^{r}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)s^{r}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+2Xs+Ys^{2})^{-\lambda}=\sum_{r\geq 0}(-1)^{r}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)s^{r},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)=Y^{r/2}C_{r}^{\lambda}\!\left(\frac{X}{\sqrt{Y}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>X</mi><msqrt><mi>Y</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)=Y^{r/2}C_{r}^{\lambda}\!\left(\frac{X}{\sqrt{Y}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Parity of the Gegenbauer polynomial makes <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> a polynomial.
More explicitly,</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)=\sum_{j=0}^{\lfloor r/2\rfloor}\frac{(-1)^{j}2^{r-2j}(\lambda)_{r-j}}{j!(r-2j)!}X^{r-2j}Y^{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)=\sum_{j=0}^{\lfloor r/2\rfloor}\frac{(-1)^{j}2^{r-2j}(\lambda)_{r-j}}{j!(r-2j)!}X^{r-2j}Y^{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda)_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda)_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\lambda)_{k}=\lambda(\lambda+1)\cdots(\lambda+k-1)\qquad(k\geq 1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda)_{k}=\lambda(\lambda+1)\cdots(\lambda+k-1)\qquad(k\geq 1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.6" class="ltx_p">is the rising Pochhammer symbol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Writing</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=q(X,Y;s)=1+2Xs+Ys^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=q(X,Y;s)=1+2Xs+Ys^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">differentiation of the left side of (<a href="#S3.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) with respect to <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{X}q^{-\lambda}=-2\lambda sq^{-\beta},\qquad\partial_{Y}q^{-\lambda}=-\lambda s^{2}q^{-\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{X}q^{-\lambda}=-2\lambda sq^{-\beta},\qquad\partial_{Y}q^{-\lambda}=-\lambda s^{2}q^{-\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\beta}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\beta}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-by-<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> Wronskian whose rows are
derivative orders <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,\ldots,2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,\ldots,2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> and whose columns, in paired order,
are the functions in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Define
<math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{2n\times 2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{2n\times 2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with rows ordered
<math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,X),(1,Y),\ldots,(n,X),(n,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,X),(1,Y),\ldots,(n,X),(n,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and columns
<math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math>, by</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{(i,X),r}=\partial_{X}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}),\qquad A_{(i,Y),r}=\partial_{Y}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{(i,X),r}=\partial_{X}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}),\qquad A_{(i,Y),r}=\partial_{Y}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact jet determinant)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the preceding ordering,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{W_{\beta}(0)=\frac{(-1)^{n}\prod_{r=0}^{2n-1}r!}{2^{n}\lambda^{2n}}\det A.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{W_{\beta}(0)=\frac{(-1)^{n}\prod_{r=0}^{2n-1}r!}{2^{n}\lambda^{2n}}\det A.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(s)^{-\beta}=\sum_{r\geq 0}c_{i,r}s^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(s)^{-\beta}=\sum_{r\geq 0}c_{i,r}s^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
<math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i,-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i,-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Coefficient comparison in
(<a href="#S3.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) yields</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i,r}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{2\lambda}\partial_{X}K_{r+1}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}),\qquad c_{i,r-1}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{\lambda}\partial_{Y}K_{r+1}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i,r}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{2\lambda}\partial_{X}K_{r+1}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}),\qquad c_{i,r-1}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{\lambda}\partial_{Y}K_{r+1}^{\lambda}(X_{i},Y_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Passing from Taylor coefficients to derivatives multiplies row <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r!</annotation></semantics></math>. The alternating signs contribute</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{\sum_{r=0}^{2n-1}r}=(-1)^{n(2n-1)}=(-1)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{\sum_{r=0}^{2n-1}r}=(-1)^{n(2n-1)}=(-1)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">The first column in each pair contributes <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\lambda)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\lambda)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
second contributes <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Transposition does not change the
determinant, and (<a href="#S3.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Lemma 3.1 (Exact jet determinant). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The factor in (<a href="#S3.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Lemma 3.1 (Exact jet determinant). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) is nonzero for every <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We next show that <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det A</annotation></semantics></math> cannot vanish.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(A singular Wronskian creates critical points)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\beta}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\beta}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a nonconstant polynomial <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>,
even in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, of exact degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\leq 2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\leq 2n</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h:=h_{xx}+h_{\rho\rho}+\frac{2\lambda}{\rho}h_{\rho}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h:=h_{xx}+h_{\rho\rho}+\frac{2\lambda}{\rho}h_{\rho}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and having the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct upper-half-plane critical points</span></p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(X_{i},\sqrt{Y_{i}-X_{i}^{2}}\right),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(X_{i},\sqrt{Y_{i}-X_{i}^{2}}\right),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\beta}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\beta}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is singular. Hence some nonzero vector
<math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{1},\ldots,d_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{1},\ldots,d_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> makes</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X,Y)=\sum_{r=1}^{2n}d_{r}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X,Y)=\sum_{r=1}^{2n}d_{r}K_{r}^{\lambda}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{X}(X_{i},Y_{i})=H_{Y}(X_{i},Y_{i})=0\qquad(i=1,\ldots,n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{X}(X_{i},Y_{i})=H_{Y}(X_{i},Y_{i})=0\qquad(i=1,\ldots,n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Set</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x,\rho)=H(x,x^{2}+\rho^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x,\rho)=H(x,x^{2}+\rho^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\sum_{r=1}^{2n}d_{r}Z_{r}^{\lambda},\qquad Z_{r}^{\lambda}(x,\rho)=(x^{2}+\rho^{2})^{r/2}C_{r}^{\lambda}\!\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}+\rho^{2}}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\sum_{r=1}^{2n}d_{r}Z_{r}^{\lambda},\qquad Z_{r}^{\lambda}(x,\rho)=(x^{2}+\rho^{2})^{r/2}C_{r}^{\lambda}\!\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}+\rho^{2}}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Each <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonzero homogeneous polynomial of degree <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:
indeed <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{r}^{\lambda}(1)=(2\lambda)_{r}/r!&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{r}^{\lambda}(1)=(2\lambda)_{r}/r!&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is nonconstant
and has degree at most <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The Gegenbauer differential equation</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-z^{2})(C_{r}^{\lambda})^{\prime\prime}-(2\lambda+1)z(C_{r}^{\lambda})^{\prime}+r(r+2\lambda)C_{r}^{\lambda}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-z^{2})(C_{r}^{\lambda})^{\prime\prime}-(2\lambda+1)z(C_{r}^{\lambda})^{\prime}+r(r+2\lambda)C_{r}^{\lambda}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">is equivalent, after writing <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=R\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=R\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=R\sin\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=R\sin\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}Z_{r}^{\lambda}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}Z_{r}^{\lambda}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; see
DLMF Table 18.8.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Hence
(<a href="#S3.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Proposition 3.2 (A singular Wronskian creates critical points). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) holds.
Finally, with <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated at <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)=(x,x^{2}+\rho^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)=(x,x^{2}+\rho^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the chain rule gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{x}=H_{X}+2xH_{Y},\qquad h_{\rho}=2\rho H_{Y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{x}=H_{X}+2xH_{Y},\qquad h_{\rho}=2\rho H_{Y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">Equations (<a href="#S3.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) and <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}&gt;X_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}&gt;X_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> show that every point listed in
(<a href="#S3.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ Proposition 3.2 (A singular Wronskian creates critical points). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is a critical point of <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.
These points are distinct because the pairs <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{i},Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{i},Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The next theorem is the local obstruction that forces the paired Wronskian
to be nonzero. The vector-field index is the Brouwer degree of the
normalized vector field on a small positively oriented circle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Critical-index theorem)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonconstant real polynomial,
even in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, of exact degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Every finite critical point of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, including every degenerate one, is
isolated. If its first nonconstant homogeneous Taylor term has degree
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ind}_{q}(\nabla h)=1-k\leq-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ind}_{q}(\nabla h)=1-k\leq-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{+}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{+}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of distinct critical points in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{+}(h)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{D-1}{2}\right\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{+}(h)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{D-1}{2}\right\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is even in <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, its derivative <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible
by <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; thus <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h=0</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial identity across the
axis, not merely an equation on <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We treat off-axis and axis
points separately, then apply the planar index sum.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Off the axis.</em>
Let <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(x_{0},\rho_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(x_{0},\rho_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be a critical point with <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, be the first nonconstant homogeneous Taylor term of
<math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h-h(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h-h(q)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The coefficient <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\lambda/\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\lambda/\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is analytic near
<math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The degree-<math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-2)</annotation></semantics></math> part of <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h=0</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore</p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta A_{k}=(\partial_{x}^{2}+\partial_{\rho}^{2})A_{k}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{k}=(\partial_{x}^{2}+\partial_{\rho}^{2})A_{k}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonzero homogeneous planar harmonic polynomial. In
polar coordinates it is a nonzero linear combination of
<math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{k}\cos(k\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{k}\cos(k\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{k}\sin(k\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{k}\sin(k\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its gradient is nonzero on
the punctured plane and winds <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-k</annotation></semantics></math> times around the origin. On a
sufficiently small circle about <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the Taylor remainder gives a
homotopy through nonzero vector fields from <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla h</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same leading-term domination holds in a punctured disk. Hence <math id="S4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is
isolated and (<a href="#S4.E27" title="Equation 27 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Critical-index theorem). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">On the axis.</em>
Translation in <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the equation, so take the critical point to
be <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Evenness gives an expansion</p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x,\rho)=\sum_{j\geq 0}b_{j}(x)\rho^{2j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x,\rho)=\sum_{j\geq 0}b_{j}(x)\rho^{2j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Comparing coefficients of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{2j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(31)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{b_{j+1}(x)=-\frac{b_{j}^{\prime\prime}(x)}{2(j+1)(2j+1+2\lambda)}.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{b_{j+1}(x)=-\frac{b_{j}^{\prime\prime}(x)}{2(j+1)(2j+1+2\lambda)}.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Every denominator is positive. Consequently, the space of even
homogeneous polynomial solutions of degree <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is one-dimensional: the
coefficient of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> determines successively all coefficients of
<math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{k-2j}\rho^{2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{k-2j}\rho^{2j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The nonzero polynomial <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in
the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (A singular Wronskian creates critical points). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> spans this space. The
first nonconstant homogeneous Taylor term at the axis point is therefore a
nonzero multiple of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the Gegenbauer polynomial <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly
<math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> simple roots in <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}^{\lambda}(\pm 1)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}^{\lambda}(\pm 1)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from Gegenbauer orthogonality
with positive weight <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-z^{2})^{\lambda-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-z^{2})^{\lambda-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the standard simple-zero
theorem for orthogonal polynomials; see DLMF Table 18.3.1 and
Section 18.2(vi) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the unit circle, the radial and angular components of
<math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla Z_{k}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla Z_{k}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> are</span></p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(32)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kC_{k}^{\lambda}(\cos\theta),\qquad-\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kC_{k}^{\lambda}(\cos\theta),\qquad-\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">With the Cartesian vector <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> encoded as <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+ib" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+ib</annotation></semantics></math>, this becomes</span></p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(33)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{k}^{\lambda})_{x}+i(Z_{k}^{\lambda})_{\rho}=e^{i\theta}q_{k}(\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{k}^{\lambda})_{x}+i(Z_{k}^{\lambda})_{\rho}=e^{i\theta}q_{k}(\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(34)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}(\theta)=kC_{k}^{\lambda}(\cos\theta)-i\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}(\theta)=kC_{k}^{\lambda}(\cos\theta)-i\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">This function never vanishes: off the axis, simultaneous vanishing would
give a multiple interior root; on the axis, the radial component is
nonzero.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We compute its winding directly. Let
<math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{q}_{k}=q_{k}/|q_{k}|:S^{1}\to S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{q}_{k}=q_{k}/|q_{k}|:S^{1}\to S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The regular value <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+i</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly
<math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inverse images. Indeed, each root <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>
produces two points with <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta=\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta=\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the imaginary parts of
<math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> at those points have opposite signs, so exactly one maps to
<math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+i</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}=a+ib" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}=a+ib</annotation></semantics></math>. At such a preimage, <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}(\theta)=-k\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta)=kb." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}(\theta)=-k\sin\theta\,(C_{k}^{\lambda})^{\prime}(\cos\theta)=kb.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\theta}\arg q_{k}=\frac{ab^{\prime}-ba^{\prime}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}=-k&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\theta}\arg q_{k}=\frac{ab^{\prime}-ba^{\prime}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}=-k&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">Each preimage has local degree <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(35)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{wind}(q_{k})=-k,\qquad\operatorname{wind}(\nabla Z_{k}^{\lambda})=1-k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>wind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>wind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{wind}(q_{k})=-k,\qquad\operatorname{wind}(\nabla Z_{k}^{\lambda})=1-k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Leading-term domination on a small circle and punctured disk transfers this
winding to <math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla h</annotation></semantics></math>, proving isolation and (<a href="#S4.E27" title="Equation 27 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Critical-index theorem). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) at an
axis point as well.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The global index sum.</em>
Let <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be the leading homogeneous part of <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Homogeneous degrees
separate in <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}h=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the one-dimensionality just proved shows
that <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonzero multiple of <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{D}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{D}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its gradient is
nonzero on the unit circle, and (<a href="#S4.E35" title="Equation 35 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) gives winding
<math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-D</annotation></semantics></math>. Uniformly for <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(36)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{1-D}\nabla h(R\omega)=\nabla h_{D}(\omega)+O(R^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1-D}\nabla h(R\omega)=\nabla h_{D}(\omega)+O(R^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla h</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero outside a sufficiently large disk, and its
winding on the boundary of that disk is <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-D</annotation></semantics></math>. All finite zeros are
isolated and bounded, hence finite in number. The Brouwer-degree excision
formula, valid also for isolated degenerate zeros, gives</p>
<table id="S4.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(37)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-D=\sum_{\nabla h(q)=0}\operatorname{ind}_{q}(\nabla h)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ind</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-D=\sum_{\nabla h(q)=0}\operatorname{ind}_{q}(\nabla h).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">We use the standard local-degree and index-sum formulation from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Sec. 6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Evenness in <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> reflects every upper-half-plane critical point to a
distinct lower-half-plane critical point. Every finite critical point,
including any axis point, has index at most <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-D\leq-2N_{+}(h)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-D\leq-2N_{+}(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is equivalent to (<a href="#S4.E28" title="Equation 28 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Critical-index theorem). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Wronskian nonvanishing)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every finite <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the full paired Wronskian is nonzero at every
point of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the Wronskian vanished at the arbitrary point normalized in
(<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (A singular Wronskian creates critical points). ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> would give a
polynomial of degree <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\leq 2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\leq 2n</annotation></semantics></math> with at least <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> upper-half-plane
critical points. By <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Critical-index theorem). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq N_{+}(h)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{D-1}{2}\right\rfloor\leq n-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq N_{+}(h)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{D-1}{2}\right\rfloor\leq n-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">a contradiction. The local coordinate was chosen at an arbitrary
projective point. Choosing the other affine chart gives the same
normalization at the point at infinity, so no projective point is omitted.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> local paired functions are linearly independent
for every <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the dimension assertion in
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Paired Haar theorem). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>; the global zero bound requires one further step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>From local to global convexity</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>The rational-normal endpoint</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, put <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\prod_{i=1}^{n}Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\prod_{i=1}^{n}Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is positive on
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and multiplication by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> identifies the paired span with</p>
<table id="S5.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(38)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}(u,v)\prod_{j\neq i}Q_{j}(u,v):L_{i}\in\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}(u,v)\prod_{j\neq i}Q_{j}(u,v):L_{i}\in\mathbb{R}[u,v]_{1}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A closed projective curve in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{RP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{RP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is called globally convex if every projective hyperplane intersects it with total multiplicity at most <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> generators in (<a href="#S5.E38" title="Equation 38 ‣ 5.1. The rational-normal endpoint ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) form a basis of the
homogeneous binary forms of degree <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently the paired curve
at <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> is projectively equivalent to the rational normal curve and
is globally convex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">A positive-definite real binary quadratic is irreducible over <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
prime in the unique-factorization domain <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[u,v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[u,v]</annotation></semantics></math>. Reduce a linear
relation among the generators modulo <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Pairwise
nonproportionality makes <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> coprime to every <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}\mid L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}\mid L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear, it follows that <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus all <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> generators are independent. The space of homogeneous
binary forms of degree <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> also has dimension <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>, so the two
spaces coincide. A nonzero degree-<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> binary form has at most
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> real projective zeros counted with multiplicity, which is the
global convexity of the rational normal curve.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Convexity persistence</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison only, we record the standard fixed-endpoint component
statement. Neither this theorem nor its
interval-endpoint convention is used in the closed-projective proof below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fixed-Spin component theorem)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\operatorname{Spin}(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Spin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\operatorname{Spin}(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LS}^{d}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LS}^{d}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of positive locally convex curves
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]\to\mathbb{S}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]\to\mathbb{S}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> whose initial Frenet frame is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and whose terminal
lifted Frenet frame is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LS}^{d}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LS}^{d}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> contains globally convex
curves, it has exactly two connected components; the globally convex curves
form one of them, and that component is contractible. If it contains no
globally convex curve, it is connected.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here local convexity means that
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\gamma,\gamma^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma^{(d)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\gamma,\gamma^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma^{(d)})</annotation></semantics></math> never vanishes, with positive
local convexity denoting the positive sign. Global convexity means at most
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> intersections, counted with multiplicity, with every ambient
hyperplane; interval endpoints are not counted. This exact fixed-Spin
formulation is recorded as Fact 1 on page 15 of
Saldanha–B. Shapiro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and as Theorem 37 of
Alves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>; both present it as a known result. The interval
proof ingredients are in M. Z. Shapiro: Lemma 1, Lemmas 6–7,
Theorems 5–6, and Corollary 1 to Theorem 6 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Lemma 7 is
needed for the boundary-oscillatory endpoint cases under the convention that
interval endpoints are not counted. Anisov treats closed projective curves,
not arbitrary fixed-Spin intervals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the anti-periodic family below, we instead use the following consequence of Anisov’s theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed-projective convexity persistence)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto\bar{\gamma}_{s}:S^{1}\to\mathbb{RP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto\bar{\gamma}_{s}:S^{1}\to\mathbb{RP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq s\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq s\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, be a continuous
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math>-family of smooth embedded closed projective curves such that, for every local lift <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\gamma_{s},\gamma_{s}^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma_{s}^{(d)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\gamma_{s},\gamma_{s}^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma_{s}^{(d)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is nowhere zero; equivalently, the curves have no flattenings.
If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is globally convex, then every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is globally
convex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">As Anisov notes immediately after Theorem 6, global convexity is open
in the <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{d}(S^{1},\mathbb{RP}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{d}(S^{1},\mathbb{RP}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> topology
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, discussion following Theorem 6]</cite>.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{s\in[0,1]:\bar{\gamma}_{s}\text{ is not globally convex}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is not globally convex</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{s\in[0,1]:\bar{\gamma}_{s}\text{ is not globally convex}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, then openness of global convexity makes <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
closed. Hence <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}=\min B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}=\min B</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Since
<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is globally convex, openness also gives <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>;
by the definition of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;s_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;s_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is globally convex. Anisov’s Theorem 6, applied after translating
the parameter by <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, gives a flattening of multiplicity at least
two on <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}_{s_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}_{s_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the nowhere-vanishing jet
determinant. Thus <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>The exponent homotopy</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an arbitrary finite <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> is immediate,
because a nonzero <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/Q^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/Q^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> has the single projective zero of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2n,\qquad d=N-1=2n-1,\qquad x(\theta)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2n,\qquad d=N-1=2n-1,\qquad x(\theta)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\beta\leq\beta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\beta\leq\beta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S5.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(39)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=\left(\frac{x(\theta)}{Q_{1}(x(\theta))^{\beta}},\ldots,\frac{x(\theta)}{Q_{n}(x(\theta))^{\beta}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}\setminus\{0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=\left(\frac{x(\theta)}{Q_{1}(x(\theta))^{\beta}},\ldots,\frac{x(\theta)}{Q_{n}(x(\theta))^{\beta}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}\setminus\{0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\beta}=\Gamma_{\beta}/\|\Gamma_{\beta}\|\in\mathbb{S}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\beta}=\Gamma_{\beta}/\|\Gamma_{\beta}\|\in\mathbb{S}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hyperplane sections of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the elements of
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to the unit circle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S5.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(40)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}(\theta)=\det(\gamma_{\beta},\gamma_{\beta}^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma_{\beta}^{(d)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}(\theta)=\det(\gamma_{\beta},\gamma_{\beta}^{\prime},\ldots,\gamma_{\beta}^{(d)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For an <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued curve <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}C=\det(C,\partial_{r}C,\ldots,\partial_{r}^{N-1}C),\qquad J=\frac{N(N-1)}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}C=\det(C,\partial_{r}C,\ldots,\partial_{r}^{N-1}C),\qquad J=\frac{N(N-1)}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Triangularity of derivative jets gives</p>
<table id="S5.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(41)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\theta}(C\circ s)=(s^{\prime})^{J}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{s}C\bigr)\circ s,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{r}(hC)=h^{N}\mathcal{D}_{r}C,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{r}(AC)=\det(A)\mathcal{D}_{r}C," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>J</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\theta}(C\circ s)=(s^{\prime})^{J}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{s}C\bigr)\circ s,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{r}(hC)=h^{N}\mathcal{D}_{r}C,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{r}(AC)=\det(A)\mathcal{D}_{r}C,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">for a scalar function <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and a constant matrix <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. In derivative
order <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the leading terms are respectively <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s^{\prime})^{j}C^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s^{\prime})^{j}C^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hC^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hC^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>; all other terms involve earlier jet columns. In particular,</p>
<table id="S5.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(42)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\theta}\!\left(\frac{\Gamma}{\|\Gamma\|}\right)=\|\Gamma\|^{-N}\mathcal{D}_{\theta}\Gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\theta}\!\left(\frac{\Gamma}{\|\Gamma\|}\right)=\|\Gamma\|^{-N}\mathcal{D}_{\theta}\Gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\tan\theta,\quad t_{0}=\tan\theta_{0},\quad s=t-t_{0},\quad c_{i}=Q_{i}(1,t_{0})&gt;0,\quad q_{i}(s)=c_{i}^{-1}Q_{i}(1,t_{0}+s)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\tan\theta,\quad t_{0}=\tan\theta_{0},\quad s=t-t_{0},\quad c_{i}=Q_{i}(1,t_{0})&gt;0,\quad q_{i}(s)=c_{i}^{-1}Q_{i}(1,t_{0}+s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\beta}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\beta}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> be the column vector of the local paired functions
in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), and put</p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{u}=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\
t_{0}&amp;1\end{pmatrix},\qquad P_{u}=\operatorname{diag}_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}^{-\beta}B_{u}),\qquad h_{u}(\theta)=\cos\theta\,|\cos\theta|^{-2\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>diag</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{u}=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\
t_{0}&amp;1\end{pmatrix},\qquad P_{u}=\operatorname{diag}_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}^{-\beta}B_{u}),\qquad h_{u}(\theta)=\cos\theta\,|\cos\theta|^{-2\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=h_{u}(\theta)P_{u}\Phi_{\beta}(s(\theta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=h_{u}(\theta)P_{u}\Phi_{\beta}(s(\theta))</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Wronskian in
<a href="#S3" title="3. Gegenbauer jets and the paired Wronskian ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is the transpose of the corresponding
jet-column determinant, (<a href="#S5.E41" title="Equation 41 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S5.E42" title="Equation 42 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>) yield</p>
<table id="S5.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(43)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}(\theta_{0})=\|\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta_{0})\|^{-N}h_{u}(\theta_{0})^{N}(\sec^{2}\theta_{0})^{J}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{-2\beta}\right)W_{\beta}(0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>J</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}(\theta_{0})=\|\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta_{0})\|^{-N}h_{u}(\theta_{0})^{N}(\sec^{2}\theta_{0})^{J}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{-2\beta}\right)W_{\beta}(0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Translation has derivative one, quadratic normalization contributes the
positive product displayed above, and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det B_{u}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det B_{u}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The complementary chart is equally explicit. If
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sin\theta_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sin\theta_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, use <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\cot\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\cot\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=t-t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t-t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}=Q_{i}(t_{0},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}=Q_{i}(t_{0},1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(s)=c_{i}^{-1}Q_{i}(t_{0}+s,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(s)=c_{i}^{-1}Q_{i}(t_{0}+s,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{v}=\begin{pmatrix}t_{0}&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad P_{v}=\operatorname{diag}_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}^{-\beta}B_{v}),\qquad h_{v}(\theta)=\sin\theta\,|\sin\theta|^{-2\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>diag</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{v}=\begin{pmatrix}t_{0}&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad P_{v}=\operatorname{diag}_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}^{-\beta}B_{v}),\qquad h_{v}(\theta)=\sin\theta\,|\sin\theta|^{-2\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=h_{v}(\theta)P_{v}\Phi_{\beta}(s(\theta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)=h_{v}(\theta)P_{v}\Phi_{\beta}(s(\theta))</annotation></semantics></math>. Here
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ds/d\theta=-\csc^{2}\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>csc</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ds/d\theta=-\csc^{2}\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det B_{v}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det B_{v}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so</p>
<table id="S5.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(44)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}(\theta_{0})=\|\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta_{0})\|^{-N}h_{v}(\theta_{0})^{N}(-\csc^{2}\theta_{0})^{J}(-1)^{n}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{-2\beta}\right)W_{\beta}(0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>csc</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>J</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}(\theta_{0})=\|\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta_{0})\|^{-N}h_{v}(\theta_{0})^{N}(-\csc^{2}\theta_{0})^{J}(-1)^{n}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{-2\beta}\right)W_{\beta}(0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, the possibly negative common factors <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{u},h_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{u},h_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> have
positive <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-th powers. Moreover <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=n(2n-1)\equiv n\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=n(2n-1)\equiv n\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so in
(<a href="#S5.E44" title="Equation 44 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) the reparameterization sign cancels the
sign from the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> numerator-basis blocks. Thus the multipliers in both
chart formulas are positive. The two charts cover the circle, including
the projective point at infinity. In each formula, <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\beta}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\beta}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes
the locally normalized Wronskian for the displayed chart. Consequently
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 4.2 (Wronskian nonvanishing). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> gives
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}(\theta)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}(\theta)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;\beta\leq\beta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;\beta\leq\beta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, while
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5.1. The rational-normal endpoint ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> gives the same conclusion at <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous and nonzero on the connected set
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,\beta_{*}]\times(\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,\beta_{*}]\times(\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, and has one constant sign.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Alves uses the positive convention. If the common sign is negative, fix
once and for all an orthogonal map <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\in O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\in O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det O=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det O=-1</annotation></semantics></math>; if it
is positive, take <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O=I</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S5.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(45)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(O\gamma_{\beta},O\gamma_{\beta}^{\prime},\ldots,O\gamma_{\beta}^{(d)})&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(O\gamma_{\beta},O\gamma_{\beta}^{\prime},\ldots,O\gamma_{\beta}^{(d)})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">throughout the exponent path. This single fixed transformation does not
change any zero set or multiplicity, because for every real linear functional <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{0}(O\gamma)=(O^{\mathsf{T}}\ell_{0})(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{0}(O\gamma)=(O^{\mathsf{T}}\ell_{0})(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>; it also preserves global
projective convexity. The orientation-reversing map <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> is not
Spin-lifted. All Frenet and Spin constructions below occur only after
(<a href="#S5.E45" title="Equation 45 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison with the fixed-<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Spin</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spin}</annotation></semantics></math> formulation, fix <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and use the positively
oriented interval <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c,c+\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c,c+\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>, reparameterized by
<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=c+\pi t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=c+\pi t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\beta,c}(\theta)\in SO(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\beta,c}(\theta)\in SO(N)</annotation></semantics></math> be the Frenet frame obtained from the
positive jet of <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\gamma_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\gamma_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> by Gram–Schmidt with positive diagonal.
Apply the fixed rotation <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\beta,c}(c)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\beta,c}(c)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to normalize the initial
frame to <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Quadratic homogeneity gives</p>
<table id="S5.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(46)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta+\pi)=-\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta+\pi)=-\Gamma_{\beta}(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The norm is <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic, and the same antiperiodicity holds for every
derivative after the fixed orthogonal and initial-frame normalizations.
In particular,
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[O\gamma_{\beta}(\theta+\pi)]=[O\gamma_{\beta}(\theta)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[O\gamma_{\beta}(\theta+\pi)]=[O\gamma_{\beta}(\theta)]</annotation></semantics></math> in projective
space.
Thus the terminal jet is the negative of the initial jet. Uniqueness of
positive-diagonal Gram–Schmidt and the evenness of <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2n</annotation></semantics></math> give the
relative terminal frame</p>
<table id="S5.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(47)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I\in SO(N)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I\in SO(N).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Define the initial-normalized frame family</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\beta,t)=F_{\beta,c}(c)^{-1}F_{\beta,c}(c+\pi t),\qquad(\beta,t)\in[1,\beta_{*}]\times[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\beta,t)=F_{\beta,c}(c)^{-1}F_{\beta,c}(c+\pi t),\qquad(\beta,t)\in[1,\beta_{*}]\times[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">It is continuous, with <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\beta,0)=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\beta,0)=I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\beta,1)=-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\beta,1)=-I</annotation></semantics></math>.
The homotopy-lifting property for the covering
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spin}(N)\to SO(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spin}(N)\to SO(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, applied with the fixed lift <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on
the initial edge, gives a unique continuous lift <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}</annotation></semantics></math> on this
parameter rectangle. Thus <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\beta}=\widetilde{H}(\beta,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\beta}=\widetilde{H}(\beta,1)</annotation></semantics></math> depends
continuously on <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and lies in the discrete two-point fiber over
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I</annotation></semantics></math>. It is therefore constant:</p>
<table id="S5.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(48)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\beta}=z\qquad(1\leq\beta\leq\beta_{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\beta}=z\qquad(1\leq\beta\leq\beta_{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.5" class="ltx_p">Thus the interval path lies in a single fixed
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LS}^{2n-1}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LS}^{2n-1}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation is not used in the projective argument below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">All required regularity is uniform on this finite exponent interval. Each
<math id="S5.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a positive minimum on the circle, and</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(x(\theta))^{-\beta}=\exp[-\beta\log Q_{i}(x(\theta))]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(x(\theta))^{-\beta}=\exp[-\beta\log Q_{i}(x(\theta))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">and all its <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives are jointly continuous on the compact
rectangle. Sphere normalization, Gram–Schmidt on the nondegenerate jet
locus, and initial-frame normalization preserve continuity. Hence the
path is continuous in every finite <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> topology, in particular the
<math id="S5.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> topology used for smooth locally convex curves in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The sphere dimension is <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2n-1\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2n-1\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation
(<a href="#S5.E48" title="Equation 48 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) records compatibility with the fixed-endpoint
formulation in <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Fixed-Spin component theorem). ‣ 5.2. Convexity persistence ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>; neither the fixed-<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Spin</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spin}</annotation></semantics></math> theorem
nor any choice of a cut or endpoint convention is used in the following projective argument.
Anti-periodicity
(<a href="#S5.E46" title="Equation 46 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>) gives a closed projective curve</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
