<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[1,\beta_{*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[1,\beta_{*}]</annotation></semantics></math>. A half-open interval
<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[c,c+\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c,c+\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is a fundamental domain (equivalently, use
<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c,c+\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c,c+\pi]</annotation></semantics></math> with its endpoints identified). Under
<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=2\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=2\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, this is Anisov’s ordinary parameter circle. Reading the
same <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> would instead traverse the projective curve
twice, which does not preserve convexity in Anisov’s convention
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Since <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, injectivity reduces to the
untransformed curve, whose first two-coordinate block recovers
<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x(\theta)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x(\theta)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the descended map is injective; the established nonvanishing
of <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E40" title="Equation 40 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>) makes it an immersion, hence an embedded closed curve.
The
determinant
(<a href="#S5.E40" title="Equation 40 ‣ 5.3. The exponent homotopy ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>) says precisely that these curves have no flattenings,
and the regularity just verified is more than the <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> continuity
needed in <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 5.3 (Closed-projective convexity persistence). ‣ 5.2. Convexity persistence ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. At <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5.1. The rational-normal endpoint ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> gives a globally convex rational normal curve;
the fixed orthogonal map and sphere normalization preserve its projective
hyperplane intersections. Hence
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 5.3 (Closed-projective convexity persistence). ‣ 5.2. Convexity persistence ‣ 5. From local to global convexity ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> makes the whole exponent path
globally convex, in particular its endpoint at <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Paired Haar theorem). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> was arbitrary, the preceding conclusion holds for
every finite <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let a nonzero hyperplane section be given.
The nonvanishing full-jet determinant implies that no nonzero hyperplane section vanishes identically, and analyticity on the
compact half-period circle <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\pi\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\pi\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> makes its zero set finite.
Global convexity gives at most <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2n-1</annotation></semantics></math> projective zeros there, counted
with multiplicity. Equivalently, one may choose a cut <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> away from the
zero set; then every zero has exactly one representative in
<math id="S5.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,c+\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,c+\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, and neither endpoint is a zero.
This choice of interval merely selects one representative of each projective zero; it is not used in applying Anisov’s theorem.
Together
with the linear independence supplied by
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 4.2 (Wronskian nonvanishing). ‣ 4. Critical indices for generalized-axisymmetric polynomials ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, this proves the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
