<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Exact cancellation</h3>

<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact cancellation classification)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The original restriction in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S6.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(50)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{\alpha}\equiv 0\ \hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\widehat{\eta}_{C}=0\ \hbox{for every }C\in\mathfrak{C}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">on </mtext><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>orig</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for every </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℭ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}\equiv 0\ \hbox{on }D_{\rm orig}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\widehat{\eta}_{C}=0\ \hbox{for every }C\in\mathfrak{C}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, its reduced extension is the zero function. Thus distinct
reduced kernels are linearly independent, including at integer and
half-integer values of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The reverse implication is immediate after grouping. Conversely, suppose
that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math>. If a class with
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{C}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{C}=0</annotation></semantics></math> had nonzero class sum <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, then as <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to a_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to a_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> its combined
term would be <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c|t-a_{C}|^{-2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c|t-a_{C}|^{-2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, while every other reduced class
would have a different pole or remain bounded there. This is impossible.
Hence all zero-height class sums vanish.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus every active class has positive height. The reduced sum <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> is real analytic on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and
vanishes on a nonempty open subinterval of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math>. Differentiating
there gives</span></p>
<table id="S6.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(51)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)}{2\alpha}=\sum_{j}c_{j}\frac{t-a_{j}}{\bigl((t-a_{j})^{2}+b_{j}^{2}\bigr)^{\alpha+1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)}{2\alpha}=\sum_{j}c_{j}\frac{t-a_{j}}{\bigl((t-a_{j})^{2}+b_{j}^{2}\bigr)^{\alpha+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The associated monic positive-definite quadratics are pairwise
nonproportional, and the right side is a paired section with
<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\alpha+1&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\alpha+1&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. This real-analytic paired section vanishes on an
open interval and hence vanishes identically on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the dimension
assertion in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Paired Haar theorem). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, its paired generators are linearly
independent.
It can vanish identically only when every remaining class sum is zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>The smooth reduced count</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S6.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(52)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a,b}(u,v)=(u-av)^{2}+b^{2}v^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a,b}(u,v)=(u-av)^{2}+b^{2}v^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The normalization in (<a href="#S6.E52" title="Equation 52 ‣ 6.2. The smooth reduced count ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>), in which the coefficient of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, permits no nontrivial scalar proportionality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proportionality)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b,b^{\prime}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b,b^{\prime}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a,b}=cQ_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a,b}=cQ_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a real constant <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1,\qquad a=a^{\prime},\qquad b^{2}=(b^{\prime})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1,\qquad a=a^{\prime},\qquad b^{2}=(b^{\prime})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for nonnegative heights the two kernels are identical.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding gives</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a,b}(u,v)=u^{2}-2auv+(a^{2}+b^{2})v^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a,b}(u,v)=u^{2}-2auv+(a^{2}+b^{2})v^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Comparison of the coefficients of <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, in that
order, gives the three asserted equalities.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Smooth signed count)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and every effective <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then
(<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Item (ii) ‣ Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) holds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each reduced class <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, set</span></p>
<table id="S6.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(53)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{j}(u,v)=(u-a_{j}v)^{2}+b_{j}^{2}v^{2},\qquad L_{j}(u,v)=c_{j}(u-a_{j}v)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{j}(u,v)=(u-a_{j}v)^{2}+b_{j}^{2}v^{2},\qquad L_{j}(u,v)=c_{j}(u-a_{j}v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The quadratics are positive definite and pairwise nonproportional by
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2 (Proportionality). ‣ 6.2. The smooth reduced count ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>. In the affine chart <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the
paired section <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}L_{j}/Q_{j}^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>β</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}L_{j}/Q_{j}^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since every <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
is nonzero and the paired generators are independent, this section is
nonzero. The projective multiplicity bound in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Paired Haar theorem). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> is
<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Restricting that count to the finite affine chart proves
(<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Item (ii) ‣ Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>); any zero at infinity merely consumes part
of the same projective budget.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>One global count in the singular case</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose at least one effective <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The domain is the punctured
set <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Write</p>
<table id="S6.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(54)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}(a,b;t)=\frac{t-a}{\bigl((t-a)^{2}+b^{2}\bigr)^{\beta}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}(a,b;t)=\frac{t-a}{\bigl((t-a)^{2}+b^{2}\bigr)^{\beta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{0}=\sum_{j}c_{j}G_{\beta}(a_{j},b_{j};\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}=\sum_{j}c_{j}G_{\beta}(a_{j},b_{j};\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For an effective zero-height class with parameter pair <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{j},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{j},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, put <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=t-a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t-a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding pole is at <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Reduction makes this
the unique singular kernel at <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and all other terms are bounded
there. Consequently</p>
<table id="S6.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(55)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}(a_{j}+s)=c_{j}|s|^{-2\alpha}+O(1),\qquad E_{0}(a_{j}+s)=c_{j}\operatorname{sgn}(s)|s|^{-2\alpha-1}+O(1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}(a_{j}+s)=c_{j}|s|^{-2\alpha}+O(1),\qquad E_{0}(a_{j}+s)=c_{j}\operatorname{sgn}(s)|s|^{-2\alpha-1}+O(1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus each pole lies outside the domain, and a sufficiently small punctured neighborhood of
every pole contains no zero of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On every component of
<math id="S6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>, the field <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is real analytic.
Every component is adjacent to an effective pole, and the asymptotic in
(<a href="#S6.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) precludes <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from vanishing identically on that component.
All zeros in the domain are therefore isolated and have finite analytic
multiplicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Choose one effective pole <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{*},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{*},0)</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficient <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For
a small nonzero real <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, replace it by</p>
<table id="S6.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(56)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{*,\delta},b_{*,\delta})=\bigl(a_{*}+\operatorname{sgn}(c_{*})\delta,|\delta|\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{*,\delta},b_{*,\delta})=\bigl(a_{*}+\operatorname{sgn}(c_{*})\delta,|\delta|\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">give every other zero-height class the height <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\delta|</annotation></semantics></math> without moving
its projection, and leave all positive heights unchanged. Let
<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> be the resulting smooth field. On every compact
<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\Subset D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⋐</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\Subset D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each fixed <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, Taylor expansion
in the projection parameter and in <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S6.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(57)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|E_{\delta}-E_{0}-\delta h\right\|_{C^{r}(K)}\leq C_{K,r}\delta^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|E_{\delta}-E_{0}-\delta h\right\|_{C^{r}(K)}\leq C_{K,r}\delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for all sufficiently small <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\delta|</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S6.E58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(58)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t)=(2\beta-1)|c_{*}|\,|t-a_{*}|^{-2\beta}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t)=(2\beta-1)|c_{*}|\,|t-a_{*}|^{-2\beta}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Indeed,</p>
<table id="S6.E59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(59)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial a}G_{\beta}(a,0;t)\right|_{a=a_{*}}=(2\beta-1)|t-a_{*}|^{-2\beta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial a}G_{\beta}(a,0;t)\right|_{a=a_{*}}=(2\beta-1)|t-a_{*}|^{-2\beta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.5" class="ltx_p">the product <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}\operatorname{sgn}(c_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}\operatorname{sgn}(c_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|c_{*}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|c_{*}|</annotation></semantics></math>, and every height change enters
through <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{2}=\delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{2}=\delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The expansion has the same positive first
variation for both signs of <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose, for a contradiction, that <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has at least <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> distinct
zeros anywhere in <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>. Select exactly <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> of them across
all components, and call this finite set <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose pairwise disjoint
compact isolating intervals</p>
<table id="S6.E60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(60)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}=[q-\varepsilon_{q},q+\varepsilon_{q}]\Subset D_{\rm red},\qquad q\in S," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⋐</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}=[q-\varepsilon_{q},q+\varepsilon_{q}]\Subset D_{\rm red},\qquad q\in S,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the only zero of <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Shrink them so
that an odd-multiplicity root has opposite endpoint signs; an
even-multiplicity root with positive first nonzero Taylor coefficient has
two positive endpoint values; and an even root with negative first
coefficient has two negative endpoint values. Let <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denote the respective numbers of selected odd, positive-oriented
even, and negative-oriented even roots.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Apply (<a href="#S6.E57" title="Equation 57 ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">57</span></a>) to the one compact set</p>
<table id="S6.E61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(61)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\bigcup_{q\in S}I_{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\bigcup_{q\in S}I_{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Because this is a finite union, there is one <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that,
simultaneously for every selected root and both perturbation signs, all
endpoint signs are preserved and</p>
<table id="S6.E62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(62)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\delta}(q)=\delta h(q)+O(\delta^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\delta}(q)=\delta h(q)+O(\delta^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">has the sign of <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, there exists <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and
every <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the endpoint signs of <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are preserved and <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\delta}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\delta}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> has the sign of <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> works for all <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and for both signs of <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Every odd root persists for either sign by its endpoint signs. If
<math id="S6.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, each of the <math id="S6.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> positive-oriented even roots has negative
center value and positive endpoint values, and therefore gives one root on
each side of its center. If <math id="S6.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, each of the <math id="S6.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
negative-oriented even roots analogously gives two roots. Choosing the
better sign yields at least</p>
<table id="S6.E63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(63)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O+2\max(A,B)\geq O+A+B=2m" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O+2\max(A,B)\geq O+A+B=2m</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">distinct roots of one smooth field, all in the selected disjoint intervals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Only finitely many nonzero values of <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> can create a collision
among the perturbed parameter pairs. Choose the sign selected above and
then choose a common <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\delta|&lt;\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> outside this finite set.
The resulting field has <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise nonproportional
positive-definite quadratic denominators, so
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 6.3 (Smooth signed count). ‣ 6.2. The smooth reduced count ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> gives at most <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math> real zeros counted with multiplicity.
This contradicts
(<a href="#S6.E63" title="Equation 63 ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>) and proves the global estimate
(<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Item (iii) ‣ Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
Notice that the proof selected roots across all
components, used one compact union and one common perturbation, and did not
obtain the global estimate by summing separate bounds over the connected components of <math id="S6.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Refined singular bound)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the singular case, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\#\{j:b_{j}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\#\{j:b_{j}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of effective poles. The preceding argument gives the sharper estimate</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{I\in\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})}\#\{t\in I:F^{\prime}_{\rm red}(t)=0\}\leq 2m-r-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{I\in\pi_{0}(D_{\rm red})}\#\{t\in I:F^{\prime}_{\rm red}(t)=0\}\leq 2m-r-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Indeed, choose any finite set <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of zeros of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with disjoint isolating intervals <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as above.
Around each effective pole choose a sufficiently small closed interval, with endpoints in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that these <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> intervals are mutually disjoint and avoid all <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (<a href="#S6.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>), <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has opposite signs at the two endpoints of each pole interval.
Convergence at these endpoints preserves their signs for both signs of sufficiently small <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> has at least one zero in each pole interval.
The same choice between the two perturbation signs as in (<a href="#S6.E63" title="Equation 63 ‣ 6.3. One global count in the singular case ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>) gives at least <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math> additional zeros in the intervals <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choosing one common sufficiently small <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and avoiding parameter collisions, Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 6.3 (Smooth signed count). ‣ 6.2. The smooth reduced count ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> therefore gives</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|+r\leq 2m-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|+r\leq 2m-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> was arbitrary, the claimed bound follows. In particular, if all effective sources lie on the line, the bound is <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4. </span>Multipole tails and the point at infinity</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the finite reduced data, choose <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so large that every binomial
series below converges whenever <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t|&gt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t|&gt;R</annotation></semantics></math>. For each reduced parameter pair
define coefficients <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{r}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{r}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S6.E64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(64)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl((t-a)^{2}+b^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha}=|t|^{-2\alpha}\sum_{r\geq 0}P_{r}(a,b)t^{-r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl((t-a)^{2}+b^{2}\bigr)^{-\alpha}=|t|^{-2\alpha}\sum_{r\geq 0}P_{r}(a,b)t^{-r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first four are</p>
<table id="S6.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(65)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{0}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{0}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(66)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{1}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{1}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\alpha a," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\alpha a,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(67)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{2}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{2}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha\bigl((2\alpha+1)a^{2}-b^{2}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha\bigl((2\alpha+1)a^{2}-b^{2}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(68)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{3}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{3}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\alpha(\alpha+1)a\left(\frac{2\alpha+1}{3}a^{2}-b^{2}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\alpha(\alpha+1)a\left(\frac{2\alpha+1}{3}a^{2}-b^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Set</p>
<table id="S6.E69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(69)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r}=\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_{j}P_{r}(a_{j},b_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r}=\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_{j}P_{r}(a_{j},b_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Then</p>
<table id="S6.E70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(70)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}(t)=|t|^{-2\alpha}\sum_{r\geq 0}M_{r}t^{-r}\qquad(|t|&gt;R)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}(t)=|t|^{-2\alpha}\sum_{r\geq 0}M_{r}t^{-r}\qquad(|t|&gt;R).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first index with <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r_{0}}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r_{0}}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, termwise
differentiation gives</p>
<table id="S6.E71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(71)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)\sim-(2\alpha+r_{0})M_{r_{0}}|t|^{-2\alpha}t^{-r_{0}-1}\qquad(t\to\pm\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}^{\prime}(t)\sim-(2\alpha+r_{0})M_{r_{0}}|t|^{-2\alpha}t^{-r_{0}-1}\qquad(t\to\pm\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.6" class="ltx_p">Thus the derivative is nonzero on both sufficiently remote tails, even when
the total charge and several lower moments vanish. If every <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
vanished, the convergent expansion would make <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}=0</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If an effective zero-height class existed, choose
one with largest center and denote its parameter pair and coefficient by <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\max},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\max},0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
By maximality no effective pole lies in
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\max},\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\max},\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, so every reduced summand, and hence <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math>, is
real analytic there. The identity theorem would extend the tail identity to
that whole component. But, writing <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=a_{\max}+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=a_{\max}+s</annotation></semantics></math>, reduction makes
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\max}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\max}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and no other reduced class has the pole
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\max},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\max},0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus every other term is bounded and</p>
<table id="S6.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}(a_{\max}+s)=c_{\max}s^{-2\alpha}+O(1)\qquad(s\downarrow 0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}(a_{\max}+s)=c_{\max}s^{-2\alpha}+O(1)\qquad(s\downarrow 0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.7" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\max}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\max}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction for every <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless
of the sign of <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, under the supposition that every <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, no effective
zero-height class can exist. All effective heights are then positive, so
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> is real analytic on <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>; since it vanishes
on <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity theorem gives <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm red}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm red}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 6.1 (Exact cancellation classification). ‣ 6.1. Exact cancellation ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> then forces <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
when <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, not all moments <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish, and <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> exists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This tail calculation makes explicit that no affine roots accumulate at
infinity.
In the smooth case this requires no additional zero-counting argument: any zero
at infinity arising from moment cancellation is already counted, with its multiplicity, in the projective bound <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The equivalences in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) follow from
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 6.1 (Exact cancellation classification). ‣ 6.1. Exact cancellation ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> together with the definition of <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the derivative vanishes at every point of <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\rm orig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>orig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\rm orig}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 6.3 (Smooth signed count). ‣ 6.2. The smooth reduced count ‣ 6. Proof of the signed line theorem ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> proves the smooth
multiplicity assertion, and the globally coordinated perturbation above
proves the singular distinct-point assertion. The inclusion in
(<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) transfers either estimate to the original
domain. Finally <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq|\mathfrak{C}|\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ℭ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq|\mathfrak{C}|\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, giving
(<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Theorem 2.1 (Reduced signed Maxwell-line dichotomy). ‣ 2. Reduction and main results ‣ Critical Points of Line Restrictions of Signed Point-Charge Potentials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
